## What it does

A [capability contract](/lore/one-capability-every-surface) gives `search` one name and one set of input and output rules.
That contract becomes an HTTP route, an [MCP](/lore/mcp-is-another-surface) tool, and a WebMCP tool.
The browser tool calls the same server handler through the reader's RPC connection.

Every reader page offers the public tools for search, reading, lore, flags, prompts, the directory, and the public learning deck.
The learning tools work on data supplied by the caller. They save nothing on the server.
The browser does not register `execute`, subscriber signup, authenticated feedback, or local devtools.

Leaving the document removes its tools.

## The standard

WebMCP is a [Community Group draft](https://webmachinelearning.github.io/webmcp/). We read it on November 24, 2026.
The reader checks `document.modelContext` first, then checks the older Chrome location, `navigator.modelContext`.
It gives each tool the same input rules that the public MCP server advertises.

[Chrome's guide](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/webmcp) lists a trial from Chrome 149 and a local testing flag.
[Chrome Platform Status](https://chromestatus.com/feature/5117755740913664) still lists the feature as proposed.
[Edge runs a separate trial](https://developer.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-edge/origin-trials/trials/0b76fe60-b266-458e-a285-04e375c0c31a) from Edge 150.

The [implementation tracker](https://github.com/webmachinelearning/webmcp/blob/main/implementation-status.md) lists ChatGPT Desktop support and a future Brave Leo release.
Mozilla records a [neutral position](https://github.com/mozilla/standards-positions/issues/1412). WebKit records [opposition](https://github.com/WebKit/standards-positions/issues/670).
A browser without the API still renders the same pages.

The Chrome trial token covers `ratstack.sh` and its subdomains. It expires March 29, 2027 at 5 p.m. Pacific.
Renew it before March 29 in the [Chrome origin-trials console](https://developer.chrome.com/origintrials/).
Replace the token in `apps/web/public/assets/webmcp-origin-trial.js`.

The reader loads that script before the app starts. Chrome requires an external script from the registered origin to insert a [third-party token](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/web-platform/third-party-origin-trials). An HTTP header or a static meta tag won't activate this token.

Edge needs its own token. Register for the [Edge trial](https://developer.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-edge/origin-trials/trials/0b76fe60-b266-458e-a285-04e375c0c31a), then set `WEB_MCP_EDGE_TRIAL_TOKEN` to the issued first-party token for header delivery.

After deploying a token, check `https://ratstack.sh/` in that browser without feature flags. In DevTools, open **Application → Frames → top → Origin Trials** and check that WebMCP is enabled.

## How to check

1. Build the reader and serve it on loopback with its `/rpc` backend.
2. Launch Playwright Chromium with `--enable-features=WebMCP`. On Chromium 158, also pass `--enable-blink-features=WebMCP` to enable the draft API.
3. Open the home page. Use the `toolchange` event to wait until `getTools()` lists `search`.
4. Run this in `page.evaluate`.

```js
const context = document.modelContext;
const tools = await context.getTools();
const search = tools.find((tool) => tool.name === "search");
const raw = await context.executeTool(search, { query: "capability", limit: 1 });
const result = typeof raw === "string" ? JSON.parse(raw) : raw;
return { names: tools.map((tool) => tool.name), result };
```

5. Check for `search` and `read` in the names and a document match in the result. There must be no `rat_*` tools.
6. Run the production bundle test. It rejects forms with an undeclared `toolname` and checks that browser code excludes devtools.
